#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065419 :tutorial/content "To solve a linear equation with variables on both sides, we bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side.\n\nFor example, let's solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n5x = 2x + 12\n$$\n\nNotice that we have $5x$ on the left-hand side and $2x$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the left-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side.\n\nThe first step is to remove the $\\color{blue}2x$ from the right-hand side. We can do this by *subtracting* it from both sides of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x &= 2x + 12 \\\\\n5x - 2x &= 2x + 12 - 2x \\\\\n3x &= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x &= 12 \\\\\n\\frac{3x}{3} &= \\frac{12}{3} \\\\\n\\frac{3x}{3} &= 4 \\\\\nx &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=4$ is our answer, and we're done.\n\nWe can check that $x=4$ is correct by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x &= ^(?)2x + 12 \\\\\n5(4) &= ^(?)2(4) + 12 \\\\\n20 &= 20✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b2c184ea-e365-5696-9066-7ea9d9b0f03f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8129 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065420 :tutorial/content "Let's now solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n9x -9= 1+4x\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we must bring all variables to one side of the equation and all constants to the other side.\n\nNotice that we have $9x$ on the left-hand side and $4x$ on the right-hand side. Since the variable on the left-hand side has a larger coefficient, let's bring all the variables to this side.\n\nThe first step is to remove the $4x$ from the right-hand side of the equation. We do this by subtracting ${\\color{blue}4x}$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9x - 9 &= 1 + 4x \\\\\n9x - 9 - 4x &= 1 + 4x - 4x \\\\\n5x - 9 &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we remove the $-9$ from the left-hand side. We do this by adding ${\\color{red}9}$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x - 9 &= 1 \\\\\n5x - 9 + 9 &= 1 + 9 \\\\\n5x &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides of the equation by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x &= 10 \\\\\n\\frac{5x}{5} &= \\frac{10}{5} \\\\\n\\frac{5x}{5} &= 2 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=2$ is our answer. We can check that this is correct by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9x - 9 &= 1 + 4x \\\\\n9(2) - 9 &= ^(?)1 + 4(2) \\\\\n18 - 9 &= ^(?)9 \\\\\n9 &= 9✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ee887dc8-c657-52b2-a4e8-358afc2d1c4c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8130 :tutorial/title "Solving Equations With Variables and Constants on Both Sides"}]}
